  "body": "This is based on the developer version of the whatwg html spec.\r\n\r\nIt is stated that the `<body>` element (along with others) is a sectioning root and has an associated heading content element with it.\r\nAn explicit `<section>` element before any heading content would give no heading for the `<body>` in the document outline.\r\n`<article>` and the like also need no `<section>` element.\r\n\r\nThe focus of this issue is if there is the need for a `<section>` element within the `<main>` element of a document. Can the `<main>` element simply be used as a sectioning type with its own heading content even though it's content model is only flow and palpable?\r\n\r\nI cannot find any example anywhere in the developer version with both `<main>` and `<section>` elements to help!\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<body>\r\n<h1>bodys heading content</h1>\r\n\r\n<!-- (a) <section> ??? is this where a section should start ? -->\r\n<main>\r\n<!-- (b) <section> ??? is this where a section should start ? -->\r\n\r\n<h1>main heading content</h1>\r\n<p>or does the main element need a section at all?</p>\r\n\r\n<!-- (b) </section> ? -->\r\n</main>\r\n<!-- (a) </section> ? -->\r\n\r\n</body>\r\n```",
